    ],
    "word_count": 52,
    "html": "<p>Marcelo Claure is a Bolivian-American entrepreneur who built his fortune through Brightstar, the global mobile-phone distributor he sold to SoftBank. A Bentley University economics and finance graduate, he later led Sprint and SoftBank Group International. He now directs investments through Claure Group and is a partner and co-chairman of Brightstar Capital Partners.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "A request for a phone number during a job interview sent Marcelo Claure into a Massachusetts mobile-phone shop. He had no phone; after talking with the owner, he offered to buy the business. He expanded USA Wireless and borrowed an idea from pizza delivery: bringing phones to customers at home within half an hour.\n\nFootball had already supplied another unexpected opening. On a flight to Bolivia after university, he met national-team leader Guido Loayza, who hired him as the country prepared its successful World Cup qualification campaign. The connection outlasted that first job: Claure later became an owner of clubs in Bolivia, Spain and the United States.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 93,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "University interview supplies both formative episodes; primary club announcement corroborates sustained ownership."
    },
    "source_ids": [
